hexagon: the chunked gated delta net and the conv chunk with the rollback slots of a draft

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus the patches before this one in patches/series.

Files:
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/gdn-step.h             a new header, one token of the delta rule
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/gated-delta-net-ops.c  that token as one function, and the output guard of the fused step
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/gdn-chunk-ops.c        the chunked kernel takes K > 1
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/gdn-conv-ops.c         the conv chunk writes K slots
    tests/test-backend-ops.cpp                       the gate of the accuracy bound, and seven cases at K > 1

The problem

Speculative decoding gives the context K recurrent rollback slots, and the app sets K = 4. The
chunked kernel of gdn-chunk-ops.c refused every K above 1, thus a prefill with speculation on ran
the sequential kernel of gated-delta-net-ops.c instead. On the OnePlus 13 with the 4B at Q8_0,
pp512 fell from 899.65 t/s to 425.08 t/s, a factor of 2.1. The conv half had the same defect: the
host matcher refuses a batch with more than one snapshot slot, because the batch kernel wrote one.

What the output must hold

The output of GATED_DELTA_NET is one contiguous f32 tensor
[S_v * H_v, n_tokens * n_seqs + K * S_v * n_seqs]. The attention region is
out[((seq * n_tokens + t) * H_v + h) * S_v + j]. The snapshot region starts at the float offset
A = S_v * H_v * n_tokens * n_seqs, and slot s of head h of sequence seq is
out[A + s * (D * n_seqs) + (seq * H_v + h) * S_v * S_v + j * S_v + i], with D = S_v * S_v * H_v.
The index i is the key index and j is the value index, thus the state is transposed, and the input
state uses the same layout. Slot s holds the state after token n_tokens - 1 - s. The op writes the
slots 0 to min(n_tokens, K) - 1 and leaves the other slots as they are. The reference of the CPU
gives that rule at ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ops.cpp:10955 and calls the other slots caller-owned.

Why a chunk gives no slot

The chunked kernel takes chunks of 64 tokens. Inside a chunk the recurrence becomes matrix products,
and the kernel keeps the state at the chunk boundary only. The states of the tokens inside a chunk
are never materialized: the kernel holds the WY form of the chunk, not the states. A state K tokens
back is thus not available at the end of the chunk, and a backward walk from the final state divides
by the decay of each token, which amplifies the error without a bound when a gate is small.

The tail

The chunk loop stops K tokens before the end of the batch, and those tokens run one by one on the
HVX. Each of them gives one snapshot slot: token n_tok_ch + s gives slot n_tail - 1 - s, thus the
last token gives slot 0. The state of the chunk boundary is already in VTCM, and phase_end
transposes it into the layout of DDR before the tail starts. The tail then reads and writes that
same buffer, and a DMA of the buffer after each token gives the slot. No transpose is necessary for
each slot.

The tail is not new arithmetic. The per-token body of the two sequential threads was the same code
twice, and this patch moves it into one function, gdn_step_token_f32. The two threads call it, and
so does the tail. The function is the code that task #74 verified against a float64 reference at
K = 1 to 4, thus the tail inherits that result.

The gate of the chunked kernel becomes the token count outside the tail: the kernel takes the batch
when n_tokens - K is 32 or more. A shorter batch runs the sequential kernel, which takes every shape.

The conv chunk

Slot g of the conv cache holds the three columns of the CONCAT that end at token T - 1 - g. The
kernel walked the shift over the last three tokens and stored one slot. It now starts the walk two
tokens before the deepest slot and stores each slot as the walk reaches it, thus K slots cost K - 1
more steps of the shift. When the batch is shorter than the slots ask for, the walk starts at token
0 from the state of the cache, which is the state of every slot that reaches before the batch. The
host clamps the window of such a slot to column 0 of the CONCAT, which is that same state.

The walk no longer calls gdn_conv_block. The convolution and the SiLU of those steps were never
used, and the new gdn_conv_shift_slot does the lane work alone. Thus one slot is cheaper than before.

The output guard of the fused state step

op_gdn_state_step demanded an output of [S_v * H, 1 + K * S_v]. The op writes one row of it, the
attention row, because the new state goes to the cache slot. The guard now validates that row alone,
thus the host can hand a view of the row and keep the dirty range at S_v * H floats. At K = 4 the
whole output is 4.20 MB for the 2B and 8.40 MB for the 4B, and both cross
HEX_L2_FLUSH_ALL_THRESHOLD of htp/hex-utils.h, which flushes the whole L2. The full output still
passes the guard, thus the host of today keeps working.

The cost

Bytes, for the 4B with S_v 128, H_v 32, one sequence: one slot is 2.10 MB, thus K = 4 writes
8.39 MB for each of the 24 gated delta net layers instead of 2.10 MB. The extra traffic of one
ubatch of 512 tokens is 151 MB. The attention region of the same ubatch is 8.39 MB for each layer,
thus the snapshots double the write traffic of this op.

Cycles, measured in the cycle-accurate simulator with tools/htp-lab (target gdntail, head size 128):
one token of the tail costs 14456 cycles for one head, which is 6.84 us at 2112 MHz. A tail of
K = 4 over 32 heads on 4 threads costs 462 592 cycles for each layer, which is 219 us. Over 24
layers that is 5.26 ms of an ubatch, against 569 ms of a 512-token prefill at 899.65 t/s.

The same measurement gives the cost of the defect: the sequential kernel over 512 tokens costs
28.0 ms for each layer on 4 threads, thus 673 ms over 24 layers, against 78 ms for the chunked
kernel. The loss that the phone showed is 635 ms.

The conv slots cost 1.6 % of the conv chunk over 512 tokens (target gdnconvslots, 8192 channels,
one thread): 3 more steps of the shift at 300 cycles for each block of 32 channels.

Accuracy

The lab target gdntail does a check of gdn_step_token_f32 against a float64 reference of the same
token. At head size 128 with the scalar gate the NMSE of the state is 1.94e-15 and the NMSE of the
attention output is 8.83e-15. With KDA the two are 7.35e-15 and 1.43e-14. The head sizes 32 and 35
cover the other two unroll branches of the function, and each of the four runs stays below 1.5e-14.

The lab target gdnconvslots does a check of every slot of the conv chunk against a scalar reference
at 1 to 5 slots, at 8192 channels over 16 and 64 tokens. Every slot agrees to the bit.

A model of the control flow on the host, with the delta rule in float64 in both the reference and
the kernel, covers what the patch changes: the split of the batch, the token of each slot, the
address of each slot, the address of each attention row, and the slots that stay as they are. Over
14 shapes with K of 1 to 32, head sizes 32 to 128, 1 to 8 value heads, a value head repeat of 1 and
2, and 33 to 200 tokens, the two models agree element by element with an NMSE of exactly 0.

The accuracy bound of test-backend-ops held the tight default at K > 1, because its predicate had
K == 1 in it. The predicate now takes the token count outside the tail, thus the bound of the chunk
covers the shapes that the chunked kernel takes at K > 1. The tail is f32, thus it is tighter than
the chunk, and the bound of the chunk holds for the whole output.

The tests

No case of test-backend-ops had n_tokens of 32 or more together with K > 1, thus no case reached the
chunked kernel at K > 1. This patch adds seven cases: the chunk count 1 and 2, a chunk that is not
full, the boundary of the gate at 32 tokens outside the tail, the shape one token below it, a value
head repeat of 2, and K = 5.

What this patch does not do

The host still refuses the conv chain of a batch with more than one snapshot slot, at
ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp-gdn-match.h:327. That line belongs to the host side. The kernel is ready
for it now.

